            "answer": "## What the retrieved context establishes\n\n- LangGraph checkpoints serialize channel values in graph state using serializer objects.\n- `langgraph_checkpoint` defines a `SerializerProtocol`.\n- `JsonPlusSerializer` is the default serializer and supports a wide variety of types, including LangChain and LangGraph primitives, datetimes, enums, and more.\n- `entrypoint` inputs and outputs, and `task` outputs, must be JSON-serializable. Non-serializable inputs or outputs cause a runtime error when a checkpointer is configured.\n- Checkpoints are persisted and later used to restore a thread’s state.\n- A custom checkpoint saver can provide its own serializer through the `serde` attribute. The implementation shows the default as:\n\n```python\nserde: SerializerProtocol = JsonPlusSerializer()\n```\n\n## Answers to the specific questions\n\n### How does LangGraph serialize and deserialize checkpoint state?\n\nThe documented behavior is that checkpoint serializers encode and decode checkpoint state, including channel values. The default implementation is `JsonPlusSerializer`.\n\nThe context does not provide the internal serialization or deserialization algorithm, nor the exact methods used for encoding and decoding.\n\n### How does `JsonPlusSerializer` handle unknown or unavailable Python types?\n\nThe retrieved context does not specify how `JsonPlusSerializer` handles unknown, unavailable, or unrecognized Python types during deserialization.\n\nIt only states that the serializer supports a wide variety of types.\n\n### What happens when a custom Python type cannot be imported or reconstructed?\n\nThis is not determined by the retrieved context. It does not document whether deserialization:\n\n- raises an exception,\n- skips the value,\n- returns a placeholder,\n- returns a dictionary,\n- or uses another fallback.\n\n### How does LangGraph handle backward compatibility after deployment?\n\nThe context does not describe a backward-compatibility mechanism for previously persisted checkpoints after an application deployment. It also does not specify whether serialized type information is versioned, migrated, or validated against the current application code.\n\n### How do type registration and imports affect restoration of custom state objects?\n\nThe retrieved context does not document:\n\n- a type-registration API,\n- how serialized type identifiers are stored,\n- how imports are performed,\n- whether classes must remain importable at the same module path,\n- or how custom classes are reconstructed.\n\nTherefore, their effect on restoration cannot be determined from the provided material.\n\n### Can deserialization fall back to a dictionary?\n\nThe retrieved context does not state that checkpoint deserialization falls back to a dictionary when the original type is unavailable. This behavior should not be assumed based on the available documentation.\n\n### Can incompatible custom state types cause corrupted or incomplete state?\n\nThe context establishes that serialization is required so state can be reliably saved and restored, and that non-serializable values cause a runtime error when checkpointing is configured.\n\nHowever, it does not document the behavior for a type that was serializable under one application version but incompatible with a later version. In particular, it does not establish whether such a mismatch causes:\n\n- corrupted state,\n- incomplete state,\n- a deserialization exception,\n- or some fallback representation.\n\nThus, the specific failure mode is unsupported by the retrieved context.\n\n## Related checkpoint behavior that is documented\n\nCheckpointing also stores node-level writes in addition to super-step checkpoints. Successful writes from nodes that finish before another node fails are persisted and can avoid recomputation when resuming. These task-level writes are not full `StateSnapshot` checkpoints; time travel resumes from full checkpoints at super-step boundaries.",
